CHAPTER IX — BUILDINGS & ECONOMY
Buildings define your strategic infrastructure.
Economy determines your ability to produce units, maintain logistics, power your systems, and unlock advanced technologies.
Together, they form the foundation of Obsidian Protocol’s autonomous warfare ecosystem.

This chapter defines:

Construction rules

Building types

Resource systems

Supply chains

Logistics nodes

Power grids

Repair systems

Production queues

Building upgrades

9.1 Construction Rules
Construction Phases
All buildings follow a three‑phase construction model:

Site Preparation

Clear terrain

Mark build radius

Deploy construction drones

Structural Assembly

Frame construction

Material delivery

Power grid connection

Activation

Systems online

Command link established

Autonomous routines initialized

Construction Requirements
Sufficient resources

Build radius within Command Core or Nexus

Power grid access (for advanced buildings)

No hazards or suppression zones in build area

Autonomous Construction Drones
Construction is performed by autonomous drones that:

Avoid hazards

Path around suppression zones

Request escort if threatened

Repair themselves if damaged

Scale in number based on building size

9.2 Build Radius
The build radius determines where structures can be placed.

Radius Sources
Command Core — primary radius

Nexus — extends build radius

Fusion — extends power grid radius

Vector Core — allows experimental structures

Radius Rules
Buildings must be placed within active radius

Radius expands with upgrades

Radius can be disrupted by EMP or hazards

Command units can temporarily boost radius

9.3 Autonomous Construction Drones
Construction drones are semi‑autonomous units dedicated to building and repair.

Drone Capabilities
Material transport

Structural assembly

Power grid linking

Hazard avoidance

Self‑repair

Emergency evacuation

Drone Behavior
Retreat when suppressed

Request escort from nearby squads

Prioritize Command Core repairs

Avoid fire, radiation, EMP zones

Coordinate with logistics nodes

9.4 Resource Types
Obsidian Protocol uses a four‑tier resource system:

Tier I — Raw Resources
Meat

Wood

Coal

Iron

Used for basic construction and unit production.

Tier II — Processed Materials
Alloys

Electronics

Fuel

Composite armor

Used for advanced units and buildings.

Tier III — Strategic Resources
Energy

Data

Command tokens

Used for command units, abilities, and experimental unlocks.

Tier IV — Experimental Resources
Vector charge

Quantum lattice

Helix substrate

Used exclusively for experimental units and structures.

9.5 Supply Chains
Supply chains connect resource extractors, logistics nodes, and production buildings.

Supply Chain Components
Extractors — gather raw resources

Refineries — convert raw to processed

Logistics Nodes — distribute materials

Factories — produce units

Command Units — regulate flow

Supply Chain Rules
Chains must be continuous

Chains can be disrupted by hazards

Chains can be extended by Nexus units

Chains can be optimized with upgrades

Autonomous Logistics Behavior
Reroute around hazards

Prioritize critical buildings

Request escort if threatened

Balance load across multiple nodes

9.6 Logistics Nodes
Logistics nodes are the backbone of resource distribution.

Node Functions
Material routing

Storage

Repair drone coordination

Power grid linking

Hazard monitoring

Node Upgrades
Increased storage

Faster routing

Hazard resistance

Autonomous repair boost

9.7 Power Grid
The power grid connects Fusion units to all powered structures.

Power Grid Rules
Buildings require power to operate

Power grid radius expands with Fusion upgrades

EMP disrupts grid temporarily

Backup generators activate during outages

Power Types
Basic Power — factories, extractors

Advanced Power — command units, research labs

Experimental Power — Vector Core, experimental units

Power Grid AI Behavior
Reroute power around damaged nodes

Prioritize Command Core

Activate emergency reserves

Request repair drones

9.8 Repair Systems
Repair systems maintain building integrity.

Repair Sources
Construction drones

Repair drones

Fusion energy fields

Command unit abilities

Repair Rules
Repairs require resources

Repairs pause during suppression

Repairs accelerate inside command radius

Repairs can be automated or manual

Repair AI Behavior
Prioritize critical structures

Avoid hazards

Retreat when suppressed

Coordinate with logistics nodes

9.9 Production Queues
Production queues manage unit creation.

Queue Rules
Units build sequentially

Queue length depends on building level

Queue can be paused or reprioritized

Queue efficiency increases with upgrades

Queue Types
Ground Factory Queue

Airfield Queue

Shipyard Queue

Experimental Lab Queue

Autonomous Queue Behavior
Adjust based on battlefield needs

Prioritize counter‑units

Request resources from logistics nodes

Delay production during hazards

